{
  "dom_id": "step-q253861",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14162617/assets/db233bb76aeb4692d932967a8a61dd7ad675a7df7adb1a006568cf12d25d5511.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?$\\,{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-\\infty ,3).$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is smaller than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=-\\infty .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
